# Portfolio review 2 — contracts, structure and build order

## Three ranked changes

1. **Finish the distinction between an entry, its published route and the places that advertise it.** `draft` and `writeup` are useful, but cannot represent all four scenarios in the brief. Add the few explicit placement decisions actually needed, scope IDs and ranking, and validate the result against fixtures before building more components.
2. **Specify theme event ordering, not just ownership.** Immediate subscription solves late arrival; it does not prevent an older queued storage event from replacing a newer local choice. Keep vanilla TypeScript, make storage notifications trigger reconciliation, and test a delayed scene subscription against deliberately reordered notifications.
3. **Reconcile the authoritative plan, then build a small end-to-end slice.** Several changes claimed in the round-two response are absent from `DESIGN.md` itself. Fix those contradictions once, replace the old short map, and move to executable evidence. Another large prose expansion will have diminishing value.

The direction is sound enough to start that slice. I would keep Astro, MDX, plain CSS and plain TypeScript. The 3D, cursors and ledger are accepted owner choices; this review concentrates on their implementation cost and behavior rather than reopening whether you should enjoy them.

Reviewed the revised `DESIGN.md`, `docs/REVIEW-2-PROMPT.md`, relevant existing source/configuration and installed Astro behavior. `docs/STRUCTURE.md` was inspected only to locate stale references, not treated as authoritative. The mockup URL remains inaccessible through the available web tool, so the reported v9 dimensions and alignment are not visually verified here. Placeholder copy and motto remain out of scope. References to DESIGN line numbers describe this revision.

## 1. Attack the content contract

### (a) A project that is also a hobby

The current model makes the folder choose both canonical identity and discovery category. Copying the same MDX into `projects/` and `play/` produces two articles that will drift, two URLs and potentially two home appearances.

Keep one canonical entry: for example `projects/keyboard/index.mdx` and `/projects/keyboard`. If it should appear in the Play index, add an explicit `showInPlay` project field when this requirement becomes real. The Play selector includes that project but links to its existing URL. Do not copy the body or generate a second article route. If the hobby story and engineering case study are genuinely different articles, separate entries with a related-content link are appropriate.

Keep homepage placement separate: cross-listing on `/play` should not automatically feature the project again in the home Play preview. Deduplicate any combined discovery list by collection-qualified identity.

### (b) Returning to the same employer

`work/carro` cannot identify two separate tenures. Use entry IDs such as `carro-2025` and `carro-2029`; both can refer to employer `carro`. Employer identity and role/tenure identity are different things.

A small `data/employers.ts` can own the shared name, website and logo if repetition actually occurs. Dates, title, summary and body stay on each work entry. Do not group employment by company and accidentally collapse separate periods. Do not reject overlapping roles globally: concurrent work can be legitimate.

If `/work/carro` has already shipped, retain its ID or redirect it deliberately. A folder-derived slug is simple, but renaming a folder is also a URL change; record this in the authoring guide.

### (c) Published article, role absent from the ledger

This is not expressible yet. `draft: true` hides the article too; `writeup: false` suppresses its route. Add a narrowly named placement field such as `showInWork`, defaulting to true. I recommend defining it to control the ledger, home work rows and featured-work slot together. A narrower `showInLedger` is also valid, but then home behavior needs a separate explicit decision.

| Entry state | Work discovery surfaces | Article route |
|---|---|---|
| `draft: true` | Absent | Absent |
| Published, `writeup: false`, `showInWork: true` | Visible, no article link | Absent |
| Published, `writeup: true`, `showInWork: true` | Visible with link | Present |
| Published, `writeup: true`, `showInWork: false` | Absent | Present |

The last row is still a public, indexable article. Include it in the sitemap and article navigation if that is the intended meaning. If “unlisted” or confidential is intended instead, that is a different requirement; do not overload `draft` or imply that hiding a link provides privacy.

This also exposes a v9 question: does “latest featured role” mean latest visible role, or latest visible role with a write-up? Prefer latest visible role. If it has no write-up or cover, the feature must remain valid without its read link or picture; do not silently promote an older employer as the latest one.

### (d) A project with a video cover

An article-only `Video` component does not solve a card/header media requirement. The current `cover` image field cannot take an MP4 and retain the image assumptions.

The simplest launch contract is **a static cover/poster on cards and social previews, with a video in the article**. That supports a video-led project without broadening every card. If the article header itself needs to render the video, introduce a small media union at that point:

```ts
type HeroMedia =
  | { kind: 'image'; image: ImageMetadata; alt: string }
  | { kind: 'video'; src: string; poster: ImageMetadata; label: string };
```

This is a conceptual shape, not a complete schema: accessibility metadata such as caption tracks or a transcript depends on the video's content. Cards still select a static poster, and social-image generation still receives an image. Reserve dimensions, disable autoplay and avoid downloading the video on a listing page. A media union is warranted because rendering behavior differs; an arbitrary `layoutVariant` is not needed.

### Remaining contract details that should be decided now

- **Identity:** use `(collection, id)`, not a bare slug, in shared helpers. `projects/homelab` and `play/homelab` do not collide as URLs. Validate lowercase kebab-case folder names and single-level `*/index.mdx` inputs rather than accepting nested paths accidentally.
- **Duplicates:** “fails the build” is not yet guaranteed. Installed Astro 7.3.5's glob loader throws on a duplicate when `prerenderConflictBehavior` is `error`; the installed default is `warn`. Configure the error behavior and test a collision. A check after entries have been keyed into a store can miss a replaced entry. [Astro conflict configuration](https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#prerenderconflictbehavior).
- **Featured ranking:** define uniqueness and scope. I recommend optional positive integer `featuredOrder`, unique among published candidates within each home section, with selectors taking the first N. A hard `1 | 2 | 3` creates unnecessary renumbering constraints. For launch, cross-listed projects remain featured only in their canonical section. Do not add a second selection list.
- **Dates/order:** name the actual project and Play ordering field, rather than merely saying “date.” A publication month is different from the start of a project or employment. Add a stable ID tie-breaker so same-month entries do not reorder unpredictably.
- **Validation levels:** frontmatter validation handles fields, build-time content validation handles bodies and references, and collection-wide validation handles uniqueness and ranking. A frontmatter refinement alone does not receive a rendered article body.
- **Nested strictness:** I tested the installed `astro/zod`. An outer `.strict()` rejected `sumary`, but accepted a nested metric containing `contex`, stripping that key. Every nested authored object requiring typo protection must be strict too. Add that fixture. [Zod object behavior](https://zod.dev/api#objects).
- **Drafts:** exclude them from output while keeping their frontmatter syntactically valid. Do not let invalid draft data unpredictably break a loader, and do not promise private asset storage merely because a route is omitted.

## 2. Is `lib/content.ts` the right seam?

Yes, as a small content-access module. Its current example signatures contradict the prose: if pages query and helpers only decide, what input does zero-argument `publishedProjects()` operate on? `featured(n)` also has no collection, and `adjacent(slug)` cannot distinguish identical IDs across collections.

Use a slightly clearer rule: **pages request content; one build-time module loads it and applies publication rules; components render supplied data**. Keep pure selection functions in that module for testing. No classes, repositories, dependency-injection framework or generalized query language are needed.

An illustrative API is:

```ts
getPublishedProjects()    // project routes/list candidates, consistently ordered
getPublishedWork()        // published work metadata, including no-write-up roles
getWorkArticles()         // published work entries with writeup: true
selectWorkRows(entries)  // applies showInWork
selectFeatured(entries, limit)
getAdjacent(orderedArticles, currentKey)
```

Choose and document whether returned arrays are fresh copies; avoid mutating a shared array with `.sort()` in different consumers. Build component props close to the page unless the mapping actually repeats.

The first thing that will try to enter `content.ts` and should not is **card presentation policy**: truncating a title to fit 638px, choosing a CSS class, generating HTML, or selecting a dark-mode logo variant. Publication and ordering belong there; rendering measurements and browser theme do not. MDX body rendering remains in the route using Astro's content renderer. [Astro content rendering](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#rendering-body-content).

Likewise, a page's metadata object is not automatically the collection's full schema. `SiteLayout` can take a title, description, canonical URL and social image without learning what a work entry is.

## 3. Three schemas, or a discriminated union?

Keep three collections and three strict schemas sharing a small set of common fields. The current folder organization and distinct date/role fields make that readable. Common should contain actual common concepts—title, summary, draft and perhaps cover—not employment dates made optional for projects.

However, the document argues against an all-optional mega-schema, not against a discriminated union. A properly strict discriminated union can reject a project's `company` just as well. It is a valid alternative, simply unnecessary here.

Use a small common **navigation projection** where collections meet:

```ts
type ArticleLink = {
  key: `${'projects' | 'work' | 'play'}:${string}`;
  href: string;
  title: string;
};
```

For launch, previous/next stays within the current collection and includes only published entries with routes. Do not point from a work story to a ledger-only role. A hidden-from-ledger public article can still participate under the decision in point 1.

Cross-collection recommendations should be explicit related links until there is a real requirement for a global reading sequence. If global chronology is added, it needs a common publication date; employment start dates and hobby dates are not interchangeable. That is a product ordering decision, not a reason to merge schemas.

## 4. Theme controller: the race is stale event data

The response says I previously concluded this is where hand-rolled state falls over. I said the opposite: two controls and a scene subscriber are small enough for vanilla code. A single owner remains the right choice.

There is no inevitable interruption between synchronous subscription steps in one JavaScript job. Registering a listener and immediately delivering current state, without an `await` in between, can be safe. [JavaScript run-to-completion](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Execution_model).

The underspecified race is between a **queued storage notification and a more recent write**. Example, with both tabs using a light OS theme:

1. Tab A is light. Tab B switches dark, then back to system/light. A has not processed the corresponding `dark` and `null` storage events yet.
2. A's user clicks dark. A updates itself and writes `dark` to shared storage.
3. A now handles the older events by blindly applying `event.newValue`. The older `null` event puts A back in light mode even though storage holds `dark`.
4. The 3D module finishes loading and immediately subscribes. It faithfully receives the wrong light state.

I reproduced that ordering in a small model: applying event payloads ended with storage=`dark`, state=`light`; rereading current storage on notifications ended with both `dark`. This is a demonstration of a possible implementation bug, not a browser test of a controller that has not been written. Storage events notify other documents, not the window that performed the write. [MDN storage events](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/storage_event).

Specify this behavior:

- Keep an in-memory preference of `system | light | dark`, plus the resolved theme. Under the selected two-state UX, selecting the current OS value means `system` and removes the stored override.
- Local commands update memory, root attributes and subscribers synchronously, then attempt persistence. Failed persistence must not undo the visible choice during that page session.
- Treat a relevant storage event as a notification to read the **current** stored preference and reconcile. Do not write back from the storage handler. Handle removal and `clear()` as well as an ordinary update; ignore unrelated keys/storage areas.
- On read failure, retain usable in-memory state; a read error is not the same as an absent key. Keep the bootstrap and controller's parsing/fallback policy identical.
- Attach listeners before the controller's initial reconciliation, so changes between head bootstrap and module startup are not lost. Reconcile on `pageshow` when returning from the back/forward cache.
- Create the scene, then subscribe; the first callback applies current state. Do not capture a theme before `await import(...)` and later overwrite the subscription's result with that stale snapshot. Unsubscribe on disposal.
- Keep scene updates synchronous where possible. If an update eventually loads assets asynchronously, use cancellation or a generation check so an old completion cannot repaint the new theme.

The intended guarantee should be immediate local feedback and eventual agreement after successful writes stop, not distributed transactional consistency. No event bus library, timestamps or framework is required for this.

**Minimum regression test:** fake storage and controllable scene loading; queue remote dark/removal notifications, issue a local dark click, deliver the stale notifications, resolve the scene import, and assert that root theme, native colour scheme, both button states, scene and stored preference are all dark. Also reverse the relevant order and check the last actual stored choice wins. Add an actual two-page browser smoke test for event wiring. Test storage failure separately, because a fake event test alone does not exercise browser behavior.

