## 6. The next gap: authoring-to-output integration

The next uncertainty is whether a real MDX entry can travel through the loader, schema, helpers, route, component mapping, styles and production server with all its promises intact. More abstract rules will not establish that.

In particular:

- **MDX integration and styles:** the existing config integrates React, not MDX. Wire the new MDX integration, local image handling and article component map explicitly. Apply article typography to the rendered MDX subtree; do not assume scoped parent styles automatically reach every child component. [Astro MDX integration](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/mdx/), [Astro styling](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/styling/).
- **Résumé ownership:** HTML and PDF now contain parallel data. For launch, maintaining the PDF manually is acceptable if an update checklist checks both. Automatic generation needs an explicit source and build tool; avoid an implied synchronisation guarantee.
- **No-JS behavior:** render ordinary links and article content first. Show all projects before filter enhancement; hide inert filter/copy controls until initialized. Let lightbox triggers be image links. Keep the 3D poster visible until a frame is ready, and preserve it after import/WebGL failure. Controls that do nothing without JS should not appear functional.
- **Production verification:** existing `nginx.conf` falls back to `/index.html`, so a missing article can return the homepage with status 200. Existing Dockerfile runs `npm run build`, not the proposed verification gate. Neither is proof of the new delivery contract. Test the built container, including direct nested URLs and unknown paths. [nginx try_files](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#try_files).

The repo contains an earlier implementation (`src/components/react/HeroScene.tsx`, flat content, React/Tailwind config). “Nothing built yet” should mean this redesign has not been implemented. Build as a deliberate migration: retain content and useful deployment pieces, replace one concern at a time, and remove old dependencies only when imports/configuration no longer need them.

For v9 card alignment, treat **638px as a mockup observation, not a production constraint**. Equal-height grid items and a flex-column card with a bottom-pushed link are sensible. Reserved per-block minimum heights can create unnecessary blank space once cards stack on mobile. Test a long title, missing cover, no metrics, zoom and font fallback. Do not truncate important evidence to preserve one artboard's measurement.

The new blanket image darkening rule is also too broad: a bright UI screenshot, photograph and contrast-test diagram need different treatment. Make dimming deliberate for relevant screenshots, preserve original media in the enlarged view, and use an appropriate logo asset/background instead of assuming a hairline border makes a dark glyph readable.

## 7. First milestone and build advice

Keep the proposed vertical slice, but add **negative cases**. One good-looking article proves very little about publication policy. Avoid implementing the entire component vocabulary first.

### A small structure I would actually build

```text
src/
  content.config.ts           three loaders and strict schemas
  content/
    home.ts                  short editorial copy
    about.mdx
    projects/<id>/index.mdx + media
    work/<id>/index.mdx + media
    play/<id>/index.mdx + media
  config/site.ts             identity, origin, navigation, socials
  data/technologies.ts       labels and optional icon mapping
  lib/
    content.ts               Astro access plus publication/selection policy
    date.ts                  validated month formatting
    icons.ts                 only if a shared adapter is needed
  layouts/
    SiteLayout.astro
    ArticleLayout.astro
  components/
    site/                    header, footer, theme button
    projects/                card, facts, filters
    work/                    row, featured role, year ledger
    article/                 Figure, Gallery, Video, Sidenote, media frame
    ui/                      only primitives that are actually reused
  scripts/
    theme.ts                 one controller and control binding
    hero-scene.ts
    bulb.ts
    cursor.ts
    lightbox.ts
  styles/                    tokens.css, base.css, article.css
  pages/                     explicit index and collection routes
scripts/                     build/content/output checks, not browser code
tests/                       content-policy and theme regressions
```

This is a destination map, not a scaffolding checklist. Create files when the slice needs them. Keep the schemas in `content.config.ts` initially; extract shared schemas only when that file becomes hard to navigate. Put article components together because the author will look for them together. There is no need for generic `Card<T>`, a component registry for every page, or a universal article service.

### Build in this order, with a concrete exit check each time

1. **Reconcile the decisions.** Patch the conflicting sections listed above, settle visibility/rank/IDs, and replace STRUCTURE with a short map and authoring instructions. Record 3D/cursors as accepted scope. Exit: one answer to each “where do I edit this?” question.
2. **Make content policy executable.** Implement the loaders, strict schemas and small content module. Use valid/invalid fixtures: published project, draft, no-write-up role, hidden-from-work public story, returning employer, nested typo, duplicate rank/ID and invalid month. These are a small number of valuable logic tests; revise “no tests beyond smoke checks” accordingly. Exit: bad content fails with a path and reason, and selectors produce the expected route candidates.
3. **Build one complete article path.** Add MDX, the shell, card, one project route, Figure, a code block, article styles and a contact link. Add the unlinked role row and narrow-screen layout. Include a second published article fixture to exercise adjacency. Exit: content renders with JavaScript disabled and all generated links resolve; draft and no-write-up URLs are absent.
4. **Add theme and verify the real serving path.** Implement bootstrap/controller plus ordinary buttons, then the event-order tests. Build and serve the same Docker image intended for deployment; check nested routes, true 404s, cache headers and failure-gated builds. Use a staging/preview destination for this milestone. Exit: both themes work before/after reload, storage failure is survivable, and a failed check cannot create a deployable release.
5. **Expand through the remaining pages.** Work ledger/feature, projects list, Play, About, HTML résumé and contact. Add components when a second concrete use justifies them. Add canonical/social metadata and sitemap based on generated public routes. Exit: a new folder appears only in the surfaces prescribed by its metadata, without editing a registration list.
6. **Add the accepted playful features one at a time.** First the 3D poster and lazy scene subscription/disposal; then bulb drag behavior; then cursors; then optional lightbox/TOC/progress polish. Check each feature's failure path, reduced motion and measured transfer/runtime cost before proceeding. Keep the command palette deferred. Exit: the site remains usable when any enhancement fails.
7. **Release verification.** Check real phone widths, keyboard/screen reader basics, long content, no-JS navigation and representative performance. Verify apex/`www` behavior, preserve old URLs, and record the exact image/revision to roll back to. Refresh the short structure guide from what was actually built.

The practical goal is a codebase whose behavior can be rediscovered from a small content module, explicit page files and a few meaningful tests. You do not need a more elaborate architecture before starting; you need the remaining decisions above to survive the first real article and the first production build.
